.TH LDAPLIST 1 "November 2026" "ldaplist 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ldaplist \- list entries from OpenLDAP and Active Directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ldaplist
.RB [ \-dlv ]
.RB [ \-h server[:port] ]
.RB [ \-M domainName ]
.RB [ \-N profileName ]
.RB [ \-a authenticationMethod ]
.RB [ \-P certifPath ]
.RB [ \-D bindDN ]
.RB [ \-w bindPassword ]
.RB [ \-W ]
.RB [ \-j passwdFile ]
.RB [ \-t ]
.RB [ \-p | \-s | \-g | \-u | \-y ]
.RI [ database [ key ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A read-only LDAP directory listing utility written in C. Supports LDAPv3, OpenLDAP, Active Directory, simple binds, and Kerberos/GSSAPI.
.PP
With no arguments, ldaplist automatically discovers usable directory settings and displays the People table. Explicit command-line settings can be used when discovery needs to be overridden. Searches normally run recursively below the discovered base DN and use LDAP object-class filters rather than requiring fixed People or Groups containers.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -d
Enable LDAP library diagnostics.
.TP
.B -l
Display the full distinguished name and every LDAP attribute returned for each entry. For example, use ldaplist -l passwd username. Multi-valued attributes are printed on separate lines.
.TP
.B -v
Verbose connection diagnostics.
.TP
.BI -h " server[:port]"
LDAP server. Port 636 selects LDAPS.
.TP
.BI -M " domainName"
DNS domain converted to a base DN, or an explicit base DN.
.TP
.BI -N " profileName"
Read /etc/ldaplist/profiles/NAME.conf.
.TP
.BI -a " method"
Authentication method: kerberos (default), simple, bind, anonymous, or gssapi.
.TP
.BI -P " certifPath"
CA certificate file or directory.
.TP
.BI -D " bindDN"
Bind DN.
.TP
.BI -w " bindPassword"
Password argument; prefer -W or -j. Command-line passwords may be visible to other local users and retained in shell history.
.TP
.B -W
Prompt for a simple-bind password without echoing it. Selects simple bind unless -a was explicitly supplied.
.TP
.BI -j " passwdFile"
Read the first line of a password file. Protect the file with restrictive permissions.
.TP
.B -t
Enable table output. Tables are the default; this option remains for compatibility.
.TP
.B -p
Display the People table. This is also the default when no selector or positional database is supplied.
.TP
.B -s
Display the SUDOers table.
.TP
.B -g
Display the Groups table. The current group listing includes only entries with a gidNumber attribute; groups without gidNumber are omitted. The members column displays member values returned by the directory.
.TP
.B -u
Display the automount map table.
.TP
.B -y
Display password-policy entries, including common OpenLDAP ppolicy and Active Directory fine-grained password-policy attributes. Does not calculate the effective policy for an individual user.
.PP
Selectors may be combined. A positional database and optional key may also be supplied, for example: ldaplist passwd username.
.SH OUTPUT
Normal listings use fixed-width, pipe-delimited tables. The default People table columns are uid, firstname, lastname, mail, and mobile. Group output includes group name, gidNumber, and members. The exact attributes available depend on the directory schema and the LDAP access controls.
.PP
Use -l with a positional database and key, such as 'ldaplist -l passwd andrew', to display the entry DN followed by all attributes returned by the LDAP server, one attribute value per line. This detailed output is intended for inspecting the complete visible LDAP entry; it is not formatted as a table.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
Kerberos/GSSAPI is the default. Acquire a ticket with kinit; the current credential cache is used automatically. For simple bind, use -D with -W to prompt without echoing the password. Simple binds should use LDAPS and trusted CA validation. ldaplist does not extract credentials from private SSSD databases.
.SH AUTOMATIC DISCOVERY
The client attempts to use available workstation configuration, including LDAP client settings, readable SSSD domain settings, the Kerberos default realm, the host DNS domain, and LDAP DNS SRV records. SSSD configuration is often readable only by root; ldaplist does not require access to it and does not read private SSSD databases. Discovery is best-effort; use -h and -M to override the server and base DN when necessary.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LDAPURI
LDAP URI or server URI setting.
.TP
.B LDAPLIST_BASE_DN
Explicit LDAP search base DN.
.TP
.B LDAPLIST_DOMAIN
DNS domain used to derive the base DN.
.TP
.B LDAPLIST_PEOPLE_OU, LDAPLIST_SUDOERS_OU, LDAPLIST_GROUPS_OU, LDAPLIST_AUTOMOUNT_OU, LDAPLIST_POLICIES_OU
Optional subtree restrictions for the corresponding searches. Without an override, searches use the discovered base DN.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ldaplist
ldaplist -g
ldaplist -p -s -g -u
ldaplist -l passwd andrew
ldaplist -h ldap.example.com -M example.com -p
ldaplist -D 'uid=reader,ou=People,dc=example,dc=com' -W -p
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/openldap/ldap.conf
OpenLDAP client configuration, when readable.
.TP
.I /etc/ldap/ldap.conf
Alternative LDAP client configuration path, when readable.
.TP
.I /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
SSSD domain configuration, when readable; private credentials are not accessed.
.TP
.I /etc/krb5.conf
Kerberos realm configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/ldaplist/profiles/NAME.conf
Optional named connection profile.
